  "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]}^{\u2032}}{{[({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2x-2}{4x({x}^{2}-9)+32} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{2{x}^{3}-18x+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{2{x}^{3}-18x+16} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[x-1]}^{\u2032}}{{[2{x}^{3}-18x+16]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}-18} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6(1)^{2}-18} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
